{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB01410 - MONDO_grouped_11786_5324_24332",
  "verbalization": "Ciclesonide, a corticosteroid, is implicated in the treatment of allergic rhinitis through its effects on vasomotor symptoms, which are a common manifestation of the condition. The evidence suggests that Ciclesonide may act as a vasomotor agent, potentially modulating inflammatory pathways that contribute to nasal congestion and rhinitis. This is supported by its indication for Vasomotor Rhinitis, a condition characterized by non-allergic nasal symptoms, and its downstream relationships to Mebhydrolin and Mizolastine, both of which are also vasomotor agents. The shared pathway from Ciclesonide to Vasomotor Rhinitis to Allergic Rhinitis indicates a possible mechanistic link, though the exact mechanism of action remains to be fully elucidated. The evidence is consistent with a role in modulating inflammatory or neurogenic pathways, but further experimental validation is required to confirm the direct relationship between Ciclesonide and allergic rhinitis.",
